11:20:13 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's actually a logical leap worth examining directly, because it's a common reasoning pattern that doesn't always hold up: **assuming what's true of a whole system must be true of every specific part within it.** **The water cycle overall does involve freezing/melting/evaporating repeatedly — that's true.** But that doesn't mean every individual water molecule, or every specific location, experiences the *same* freeze-melt-refreeze cycle in place. The whole system can be "freezing and thawing constantly" while still meaning: - Water melts in location A - Runs off, evaporates, gets rained out somewhere completely different (location B, C, or D) - Refreezes as new snow somewhere else entirely — maybe a different mountain, maybe the same one, maybe not at all, maybe next year, maybe in a decade Global freezing/thawing being constant is compatible with **any specific glacier losing more than it gains**, because the water doesn't have to "return" to its original spot for the overall water cycle to keep functioning. The cycle can churn along at a planetary level while individual glaciers, particular mountains, or specific ice sheets shrink, grow, or vanish entirely — the system doesn't owe any specific location a return trip. **Simple example that shows why this logic doesn't scale down:** The global carbon cycle involves plants absorbing CO2 and other processes releasing it — true for the whole planet. But that doesn't mean *every specific tree* absorbs exactly as much CO2 as gets released near it. Some individual trees/forests are net carbon absorbers; some regions are net emitters. The system-level truth doesn't guarantee the same balance at every specific location. Same logic here: "the water cycle overall involves lots of freezing and melting" is true — but that doesn't mean *this specific glacier* is guaranteed to get back what it individually lost. That's the part that has to be measured locally, glacier by glacier, which is exactly why the mass balance tracking (satellites, cores, etc.) exists — because the global cycle being real doesn't settle what happens to one specific ice mass.
